# Migration SQL is checksummed byte-for-byte: backend/scripts/migration-safety-check.js holds a
# list of historical migrations with their SHA-256 and refuses any change to them, because an
# already-applied migration that is edited afterwards silently diverges from what production ran.
#
# With core.autocrlf=true — the Windows default — those files check out with CRLF, so every hash
# differs from the recorded one and the check reports migrations as "modified" on a completely
# clean tree (000062_bill_discount_reason and 000062_bill_item_hsn_snapshot did exactly that).
# The check is then unrunnable on Windows, which is worse than it sounds: it is the guard that
# would catch a genuine edit to an applied migration.
#
# Every tracked .sql file is already stored as LF, so this pins the working copy to what is
# committed. It changes checkout only, never content.
*.sql text eol=lf
